{"authored" [{"question" "q-316598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The unit square $D$ has vertices $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$ What is the area of the image of $D$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ with the standard matrix $T,$ given below?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$\\text{Area}(T(D))=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/assets/6805467a24cb40559f205cbfcecc4f5a34ff2ec6ebda8ae67f997c02f123b53c.png)\n\nRecall that for any linear transformation $T,$ we have the following connection between the area of $D$ and the area of the image $T(D)$ under the action of $T:$\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(D))=|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we get \n\n$$\ndet(T)=(-4)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 1=-12-2=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(D)) & =|det(T)|\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =|\\,-14\\,|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/state.json" "file_sha256" "4f176cdba2709a722aa9407779400d5dce2b1d5929a4c932d1d9e78dbfff94ad" "basis" 1183 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014125/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-316598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.